p: .
t. 
|  A good example of formal answers to the second question is
|  <!!Bolt et. al.!!> [/Bolt/], based on Peter <!!Gardenfors!!>'s Conceptual Space
|  theory, alongside a syntactic approach related to Categorial Grammar
.  and mathematical Category Theory


--- Sentence/switch
id: 292
r#  52674  967  34


--- Sentence//end
id: 292
r#  52969  972  27
p: .
t. 
|   Consider <!!"!!>red box<!!"!!>, and
|  (for sake of argument, although this deviates from the
|  specifics of <!!Gardenfors!!>-inspired work on conceptualization)
|  define the concepts set-theoretically:
|  red means the set of red things; box means the set of
.  boxes (or box-like things)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 293
r#  52970  972  28


--- Sentence//end
id: 293
r#  53039  973  37
p: .
t. 
|   Combining the concepts yields
.  an intersection between the two sets


--- Sentence/switch
id: 294
r#  53040  973  38


--- Sentence//end
id: 294
r#  53205  976  30
p: .
t. 
|   Meanwhile,
|  syntactically, that compositional motif is cued by the left-adjacency of
|  red before box, which in English binds
.  adjectives to nominal targets


--- Sentence/switch
id: 295
r#  53206  976  31


--- Sentence//end
id: 295
r#  53314  978  38
p: .
t. 
|   Thus we have a
|  syntactic operation/relation (left-adjacency) and a
.  semantic operation (set-intersection)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 296
r#  53315  978  39


--- Sentence//end
id: 296
r#  53544  982  30
p: .
t. 
|   The gist
|  of [/Bolt/] is that mapping the phrase to its signification
|  depends not just on mapping the two words but also
|  mapping the intra-Category morphisms to each other (adjacency
.  projects to set-intersection)

--- Sentence/end 
id: 296
r#  53545  982  31

