p: .
t. 
|  If we abstract away word-order (the left/right adjacency distinction),
|  Categorial Grammar resembles a kind of type-theoretic syntax
|  where parts of speech may be likened to a system of types,
.  such as nouns and propositions


--- Sentence/switch
id: 307
r#  55606  1020  32


--- Sentence//end
id: 307
r#  55772  1023  39
p: .
t. 
|   Because mappings from a tuple
|  of types to some result type are themselves a type,
|  we can build an arbitrarily large system
.  out of a small basis (e.g., )


--- Sentence/switch
id: 308
r#  55773  1023  40


--- Sentence//end
id: 308
r#  55886  1026  8
p: .
t. 
|  Propositions are an overarching sentential type,
|  whereas phrases may have intermediate types between
.  


--- Sentence/switch
id: 309
r#  55887  1026  9


--- Sentence//end
id: 309
r#  56081  1030  37
p: .
t. 
|   A sentence is properly
|  formed if those intermediates resolve to propositionality,
|  via type-inference rules like reading (intransitive)
|  finite-verb/noun
.  pairs as producing a grounded clause

--- Sentence/end 
id: 309
r#  56082  1030  38

