r#  66764  1251  33
p: .
t. 
|  Among the added complications relevant to a real-world push/transfer
|  implementation are -=- first -=- resolving polymorphically
.  <!!"!!>overloaded<!!"!!> function symbols


--- Sentence/switch
id: 376
r#  66765  1251  34


--- Sentence//end
id: 376
r#  66870  1252  59
p: .
t. 
|   By stipulation, the interpreter or compiler
.  has access to a list mapping procedure names to signatures


--- Sentence/switch
id: 377
r#  66871  1252  60


--- Sentence//end
id: 377
r#  66990  1254  46
p: .
t. 
|  Selecting a correct overload is straightforward if there is one exact
.  signature-match given the current stack types


--- Sentence/switch
id: 378
r#  66991  1254  47


--- Sentence//end
id: 378
r#  67131  1256  38
p: .
t. 
|   However, most modern
|  programming environments allow implicit type-casts (e.g., any integer is also
.  a decimal, with fractional part zero)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 379
r#  67132  1256  39


--- Sentence//end
id: 379
r#  67317  1259  9
p: .
t. 
|   Moreover, modern languages commonly
|  adopt template-metaprogramming and/or class-inheritance systems wherein
|  a given variable-symbol could have different types in different
.  contexts


--- Sentence/switch
id: 380
r#  67318  1259  10


--- Sentence//end
id: 380
r#  67442  1260  68
p: .
t. 
|   In these systems, to compute which overload matches a
.  given sequence of parameter-values is considerably more complicated

--- Sentence/end 
id: 380
r#  67443  1260  69

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 70
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 71
r#  67445  1262  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 381
r#  67445  1262  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 381
r#  67772  1266  43
p: .
t. 
|  A second difficulty for push/transfer mechanics is deriving the correct
|  sequence for presenting symbols to the <!!"!!>interpreter<!!"!!> (in real life, said
|  interpreter is more like an abstract representation guiding the compilation
|  of high-level code, but we can still reason about source
.  code as if it were interpreted on-the-fly)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 382
r#  67773  1266  44


--- Sentence//end
id: 382
r#  67915  1268  39
p: .
t. 
|   Usually this sequence is
|  different from the order in which tokens appear in source code, so symbols
.  have to be reordered for push/transfer


--- Sentence/switch
id: 383
r#  67916  1268  40


--- Sentence//end
id: 383
r#  68148  1271  41
p: .
t. 
|   Compilers must also deduce which tokens
|  are associated with which function-symbols, a process that involves
|  both syntax (e.g., nested parentheses) and semantics (e.g., order of operands,
.  like multiplication preempting addition)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 384
r#  68149  1271  42


--- Sentence//end
id: 384
r#  68494  1275  83
p: .
t. 
|   Parsing high-level code is therefore a
|  matter of reducing source files (which have extra structures such as nested
|  expressions) to a simpler representation based on token-sequences
|  (details like nesting become <!!"!!>flattened<!!"!!> to a single lineup, which works
.  because subroutines enforce a contract about how the program stack should be used)

--- Sentence/end 
id: 384
r#  68495  1275  84

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 71
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 72
r#  68498  1277  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 385
r#  68498  1277  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 385
r#  68625  1278  53
p: .
t. 
|  Despite its simplistic provenance, the <!!"!!>push/transfer<!!"!!> execution model
.  has some interesting resonance with natural language


--- Sentence/switch
id: 386
r#  68626  1278  54


--- Sentence//end
id: 386
r#  68865  1282  18
p: .
t. 
|   For one
|  thing, the syntactic process just described -=- parsing more-complex
|  code down to symbols arranged in a sequence ordered for push and transfer
|  actions -=- has some schematic resemblance to the role of syntax
.  in human language


--- Sentence/switch
id: 387
r#  68866  1282  19


--- Sentence//end
id: 387
r#  69063  1285  20
p: .
t. 
|   Moreover, semantic complexities such as
|  function polymorphism recall phenomena like multiple word-senses,
|  where lexical units are, in effect, <!!"!!>overloaded<!!"!!> with divergent
.  meanings by context

--- Sentence/end 
id: 387
r#  69064  1285  21

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 72
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 73
r#  69068  1287  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 388
r#  69068  1287  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 388
r#  69349  1291  19
p: .
t. 
|  Both computer code and natural language are, by many lights,
|  better represented in terms of parse-graphs than trees
|  (particularly in so far as some relations, like anaphora in the latter context
|  and side-effect propagation in the former, cut across tree-like
.  directed branches)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 389
r#  69350  1291  20


--- Sentence//end
id: 389
r#  69463  1292  64
p: .
t. 
|   With proper edge-filtering, tree <!!"!!>visiting<!!"!!>
.  algorithms (e.g., push/transfer) can be adopted to parse graphs


--- Sentence/switch
id: 390
r#  69464  1292  65


--- Sentence//end
id: 390
r#  69596  1295  21
p: .
t. 
|  Type-checking may be described as a graph-traversal
|  strategy where the <!!"!!>program stack<!!"!!> holds types rather
.  than concrete values


--- Sentence/switch
id: 391
r#  69597  1295  22


--- Sentence//end
id: 391
r#  69900  1301  9
g. 
|   @l(-%>>) +69622/1296/14
|   +69644/1296/36:16=69660/1296/52
|   +69649/1296/41:0=69649/1296/41
.   @l() -69649/1296/41
p: .
t. 
|   Assuming
|  polymorphism, a type-checker
|  must confirm that, given any stack-state with a
|  procedure symbol on top, there is a unique implementation
|  whose parameter-types match those symbolized lower on the stack
|  (if so, its output type is then placed on the stack
.  instead)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 392
r#  69901  1301  10


--- Sentence//end
id: 392
r#  70367  1309  67
p: .
t. 
|   Both type-checking
|  and push/transfer may thereby be modeled
|  as versions of a generic graph-traversal pattern, whose
|  central operations are crossing to unvisited child nodes;
|  pushing leaf-nodes' values (or types, etc.) onto an
|  external stack; and performing some external operation
|  on non-leaf nodes once all descendent branches have been explored
|  (in effect, navigating graphs with a state
.  machine holding stack-memory and a trace of previously-seen nodes)

--- Sentence/end 
